    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "Hello and thanks for this great editor!\n\n### Problem\n\nOn Windows, opening a file through a full path with `..` in it can create a second buffer for a file that's already open. Then I have two buffers with different edits for the same file, and saving one overwrites the other.\n\nI hit this with the quickfix list. A tool prints error locations like `C:/proj/build/../src/foo.txt`, and jumping to an error opens a new buffer instead of using the one I already have open.\n\nIt only happens after a `:w`. Two things seem to go wrong together:\n\n1. On Windows the `..` isn't removed from full paths, so the buffer names don't match.\n2. Then it falls back to comparing file ids. But `:w` gives the file a new id and the old buffer keeps its old one, so that check fails too.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Code I think is involved</summary>\n\n`fix_fname` sees the `..` but calls `FullName_save` with `force = false`, even though its doc says it resolves things like `dir/../subdir`:\n\nhttps://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/9b7ec59580fddf671f7bf922f66ef114a59a51ee/src/nvim/path.c#L1795-L1820\n\nWith `force = false`, `path_to_absolute` skips paths that are already absolute, so the `..` stays:\n\nhttps://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/9b7ec59580fddf671f7bf922f66ef114a59a51ee/src/nvim/path.c#L2309-L2314\n\nThe file id fallback only refreshes the buffer's id when the ids already match, so an outdated id never gets fixed here:\n\nhttps://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/9b7ec59580fddf671f7bf922f66ef114a59a51ee/src/nvim/buffer.c#L3287-L3301\n\n</details>\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n    mkdir test\\src test\\build\n    echo hello > test\\src\\foo.txt\n    cd test\n    nvim --clean src\\foo.txt\n    :w\n    :exe 'edit' getcwd() .. '/build/../src/foo.txt'\n    :ls\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nOne buffer. Instead `:ls` shows two:\n\n    1 #h   \"src\\foo.txt\"                    line 1\n    2 %a   \"~\\test/build/../src/foo.txt\"    line 1\n\nWithout the `:w` it works fine. `:set backupcopy=yes` also avoids it.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.12.0. The code above is the same on master.\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nDidn't test\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nWindows 10 Pro 19045\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nNeovide, also happens with `--headless`\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nnot set\n\n### Installation\n\nwinget\n\nThank you for your support!",
